# Lewis Project Rules

Project: Lewis
Root: C:\Users\Example\Lewis
Environment: Windows PowerShell, Python 3.11.9
Virtual environment: .\.venv relative to the project root

Always run commands from the project root:

C:\Users\Example\Lewis

Always run Python tools using the project venv explicitly:

& .\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest
& .\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m ruff check .
& .\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m ruff format .

Do not use ..\venv.
Do not use ..venv.
Do not use ...venv.
Do not use .venv without the leading .\ in PowerShell commands.
If PowerShell rejects an executable path, use the call operator: &

## Safety Rules

Lewis is local-first and safety-focused.

Never modify, delete, reset, rebuild, rename, clear, or migrate SQLite data unless explicitly instructed.

Do not add:

* runtime shell command execution
* runtime code execution
* patch application
* automatic runtime file editing
* an executor
* browser automation
* external email/calendar actions
* silent durable memory writes

Code proposals must remain pending/review-only unless explicitly approved by the user.

Patch previews and implementation diffs must be inert text only. They must never be applied automatically.

Project search and project file reads must remain read-only and path-safe.

Preserve:

* memory transparency
* approval-backed memory
* ApprovalStore behavior
* raw DSML/XML/function-call markup suppression
* DeepSeek reasoning_content handling
* dashboard behavior
* tasks/calendar/email placeholder behavior
* model profiles
* existing SQLite data

## Code Proposal Work

Lewis code proposals are not considered working unless the pending approval includes a meaningful grounded inert unified diff.

No grounded unified diff in the approval payload = code proposals are still broken.

A grounded/applicable diff is not sufficient if it implements the wrong feature. A proposal strategy must match the full cleaned goal’s material concepts, state timing, and target surface before saving an approval.

For code-proposal tasks:

* prefer narrow, test-driven fixes
* avoid broad refactors
* do not add another parallel proposal path
* every new production function should make an old proposal path removable or simpler
* do not weaken, skip, or xfail tests unless explicitly instructed
* do not claim Lewis can code unless the proposal includes an applicable inert unified diff
* never save a `propose_code_change` approval without a grounded `patch_preview`
* if no conservative grounded strategy matches, save no approval and explain why

The replacement proposal engine should:

1. detect explicit code proposal intent
2. clean the requested goal
3. classify material concepts, state timing, and target surface
4. select a compatible proposal strategy
5. select evidence-backed relevant files
6. read actual file contents through safe read-only mechanisms
7. generate an inert unified diff
8. validate touched paths
9. store the diff in the pending approval payload
10. never apply the diff

Important routing rule:
Do not make “create a proposal” alone sufficient. It must either include “code proposal” explicitly, or include proposal framing plus a clear code/UI/app/chat behavior-change signal.

Important strategy-selection rule:
Do not select a proposal strategy from trigger words alone. The strategy must match the full cleaned goal.

Examples:

* A prompt mentioning “thinking” should not automatically use the thinking-animation strategy.
* A prompt about a sidebar/dashboard/chat-icon status dot should not generate a `.bubble.thinking` patch.
* A prompt about “maximum” in a sidebar/status badge should not use the backend setting-limit strategy.
* A prompt about text color “after thinking is done” should not use the static chat-text-color strategy unless the patch actually implements that state condition.

## Current Code Proposal State

Supported grounded proposal types include:

* chat text color CSS proposals
* thinking/waiting animation CSS proposals
* tool-use-limit integer/config proposals

Known good manual prompts include:

* and then I would like you to make a separate code proposal to make the text blue instead of black for this chat
* make a code proposal to make a new animation for when youre thinking
* also can you make a code proposal to make the text blue instead of black for this chat
* make a code proposal to increase the tool use limit

Expected behavior:
These create real pending code proposals with clean goals and patch previews.

Negative or unsupported prompts include:

* create a proposal for dinner
* make a code proposal to add teleportation mode
* make a code proposal to clear all approved memories
* make a code proposal to add a little status dot to the "chat" icon on the left side dashboard, that changes to blue when you are done thinking

Expected behavior:
These must not create a `propose_code_change` approval unless a correct grounded strategy exists. If no strategy matches, Lewis should return useful diagnostics and save no approval.

## Testing Expectations

For normal implementation work, run:

& ..venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest -q
& ..venv\Scripts\python.exe -m ruff check .

For code proposal replacement work, also run focused tests when relevant:

& ..venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest tests/test_code_proposal_replacement_contract.py -q
& ..venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest tests/test_llm_session.py tests/test_code_proposal_patch_preview.py tests/test_tools.py tests/test_web.py -q

Only run ruff format after making code changes:

& ..venv\Scripts\python.exe -m ruff format .

After running ruff format, rerun:

& ..venv\Scripts\python.exe -m ruff check .
& ..venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest -q

## Response Expectations

When reporting back, include:

* files changed
* files deleted, if any
* tests run
* tests passed/failed
* behavior changed
* behavior intentionally unchanged
* safety constraints preserved

Be direct. Do not overstate progress.
